{
  "version": "2.21",
  "children": [
    {
      "type": "context",
      "id": "XrTc8",
      "x": 0,
      "y": 56236,
      "name": "Q&A · Plan interview — inspiration",
      "width": 340,
      "height": 0,
      "content": "Q&A · Plan interview — inspiration\n\nInspiration, dissected:\n• TurboTax's tax interview: one plain-language question at a time. A checklist first, then a short deep-dive per checked item. Progress by section in a side nav, never by question count. And the refund meter moves with every answer, so each question feels worth answering.\n• NYT interactives (the dialect quiz, the rent-vs-buy calculator): the payoff sits beside the question and updates instantly, with a plain-words explanation next to the number.\n• An advisor's fact-find: good advisors reflect back what they heard (\"So you're 52, with a partner…\"). It builds trust and makes corrections effortless.\n• Typeform: one big question, one primary action, Enter to continue.\n\nWhat changes from Question cards:\n• Answered questions collapse into the \"What I know so far\" sentence. Each answer is a token; click it to reopen that question in place. The page stops growing as you answer.\n• One question is in focus, as an open section with the active-step left rule (the Plan Step pattern), not a card.\n• Follow-ups stay real questions, shown as a topic track (\"Outside income · 2 of 3\") instead of an indented thread.\n• Progress is by section (About you · Income · Savings & debt · Goals · Review) with time left, so added follow-ups never move the goalposts.\n• \"Your plan, so far\" shows the live result and the change from the last answer. \"Why we ask\" explains the current question with numbers (start at 67 vs 70), which also answers the chat's \"Why do you need this?\" chip.\n• Keyboard: Enter continues; number keys pick options.\n\nMobile: the rail becomes a section strip at the top and a pinned \"Your plan, so far\" bar above Continue, in the thumb zone.\n\nTrade-offs: the sentence needs good copy for every answer type (the model writes it). Without the dimmed history cards, the underline and the hint have to carry \"you can change this\"."
    },
